Relevant columns & why they matter  
• `distract_20YY.driver_distracted_by_name` : filter out “Not Distracted”, “Unknown if Distracted”, “Not Reported”.  
• `distract_20YY.state_number / state_name , consecutive_number , vehicle_number` : key‑fields that identify the crash (state + consecutive_number) and allow de‑duping to 1 crash even when several distracted drivers/vehicles are involved.  
• `accident_20YY.state_number / state_name , consecutive_number` : 1 row per crash – use to be sure we are counting crashes, not vehicles; also gives a reliable `state_name`.  
• `population_by_zip_2010.zipcode , population` : 2010 Census population at ZIP level – base for denominator.  
• `utility_us.zipcode_area.zipcode , state_name` : converts ZIP to state so we can roll 2010 population up to state.  
Conditions you will need  
driver_distracted_by_name NOT IN ('Not Distracted','Unknown if Distracted','Not Reported')  
When joining the two ZIP tables make both ZIP codes 5‑character strings (LPAD).  

Below are 10 incremental SQL snippets (all LIMIT 20 to stay light) that explore / build up what is needed.  
```sql
-- 1. Description:   See every unique distraction label for 2015
SELECT DISTINCT driver_distracted_by, driver_distracted_by_name
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.distract_2015`
ORDER BY driver_distracted_by;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description:   Same list for 2016
SELECT DISTINCT driver_distracted_by_name
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.distract_2016`
ORDER BY driver_distracted_by_name
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description:   How many distraction records (vehicle‑level) each state had in 2015
SELECT state_name,
       COUNT(*) AS vehicle_level_rows
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.distract_2015`
GROUP BY state_name
ORDER BY vehicle_level_rows DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description:   All crashes in 2015 that had ANY distracted driver (rough cut, no de‑dupe)
SELECT a.state_name,
       a.consecutive_number
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.accident_2015` AS a
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.distract_2015` AS d
  ON   a.state_number = d.state_number
 AND   a.consecutive_number = d.consecutive_number
WHERE  d.driver_distracted_by_name NOT IN ('Not Distracted',
                                           'Unknown if Distracted',
                                           'Not Reported')
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description:   Distinct crash count by state (2015) after exclusions
SELECT a.state_name,
       COUNT(DISTINCT a.consecutive_number) AS distracted_crashes_2015
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.accident_2015`  AS a
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.distract_2015`  AS d
  ON   a.state_number = d.state_number
 AND   a.consecutive_number = d.consecutive_number
WHERE  d.driver_distracted_by_name NOT IN ('Not Distracted',
                                           'Unknown if Distracted',
                                           'Not Reported')
GROUP BY a.state_name
ORDER BY distracted_crashes_2015 DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description:   Build 2010 population per state (zip→state roll‑up)
SELECT z.state_name,
       SUM(p.population) AS pop_2010
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.census_bureau_usa.population_by_zip_2010` AS p
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.utility_us.zipcode_area`                 AS z
  ON   LPAD(p.zipcode,5,'0') = z.zipcode
GROUP BY z.state_name
ORDER BY pop_2010 DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description:   Same crash count logic for 2016
SELECT a.state_name,
       COUNT(DISTINCT a.consecutive_number) AS distracted_crashes_2016
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.accident_2016` AS a
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.distract_2016` AS d
  ON   a.state_number = d.state_number
 AND   a.consecutive_number = d.consecutive_number
WHERE  d.driver_distracted_by_name NOT IN ('Not Distracted',
                                           'Unknown if Distracted',
                                           'Not Reported')
GROUP BY a.state_name
ORDER BY distracted_crashes_2016 DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description:   Combine population with 2015 crash counts and compute rate per 100k
WITH pop AS (
  SELECT z.state_name, SUM(p.population) AS pop_2010
  FROM   `bigquery-public-data.census_bureau_usa.population_by_zip_2010` p
  JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.utility_us.zipcode_area` z
    ON   LPAD(p.zipcode,5,'0') = z.zipcode
  GROUP BY z.state_name
),
crash15 AS (
  SELECT a.state_name, COUNT(DISTINCT a.consecutive_number) AS crashes15
  FROM   `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.accident_2015` a
  JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.distract_2015` d
    ON   a.state_number = d.state_number
   AND   a.consecutive_number = d.consecutive_number
  WHERE  d.driver_distracted_by_name NOT IN ('Not Distracted',
                                             'Unknown if Distracted',
                                             'Not Reported')
  GROUP BY a.state_name
)
SELECT c.state_name,
       crashes15,
       pop_2010,
       100000 * crashes15 / pop_2010 AS rate_per_100k_2015
FROM   crash15 c
JOIN   pop     p USING (state_name)
ORDER BY rate_per_100k_2015 DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description:   Same calculation for 2016
